PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 10–12, 2–4, 6 and 8 correctly note that the host-genotype gap appears only in the ΔAΔB double mutant, yet that observation still leaves the decisive single-mutant pattern unaddressed. Only removal of C produces identical loads (3000/ml) in both wtL and –xyL, erasing every detectable difference between the two host lines. That clean equalization occurs precisely when C is absent and is the textbook signature that C’s normal activity is required to neutralize the xy product. Once C is gone, xy-mediated restriction becomes visible uniformly, regardless of whether A or B remain.  

The ΔAΔB difference, by contrast, is seen only while C is still present; when C is also removed in the triple mutant the gap collapses in –xyL and the additional drop to 1000/ml occurs solely in wtL. This indicates that A and B act in a compensatory or downstream manner once C’s primary anti-xy function is lost, not as independent direct antagonists of xy. If B (or A) were the main xy-neutralizing activities, the host-genotype differential should persist after C deletion, yet it does not.  

Option E therefore accounts for both the decisive single-ΔC equalization and the additive, xy-dependent gap that emerges only in the triple-mutant background when C is absent. F’s attribution of direct xy antagonism to B (or A) therefore over-interprets a secondary double-mutant phenotype while under-weighting the fact that the cleanest and most consistent xy signal is tied specifically to removal of C. The data continue to favor the narrower, C-centric reading in E.}

